<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>The Flagship | Feels Like Om</title>
    <meta name="description" content="The Flagship — a small-group field immersion in West Sikkim with community learning, reflection and a 4D3N mountain trek." />
  </head>

  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href="/">Feels Like Om</a>
      <nav class="main-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="/about/our-story">ABOUT</a>
        <a class="active" href="/journeys">JOURNEYS</a>
        <a href="/reflections">REFLECTIONS</a>
        <a href="/contact">CONTACT</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-image" role="img" aria-label="A person looking across the mountains of West Sikkim beneath a dramatic sky"></div>
        <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">West Sikkim | Field Immersion | 4D3N Trek</p>
          <h1>The Flagship</h1>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="journey-details" aria-label="Upcoming Flagship dates">
        <div class="journey-details-inner">
          <div class="dates-list">
            <p><strong>Nov 22 - Dec 2, 2026</strong></p>
            <p><strong>Dec 22 - Jan 1, 2027</strong></p>
          </div>
          <a class="button gentle-cta" href="#enquire">Enquire about The Flagship</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="opening-quote" aria-label="Opening reflection">
        <blockquote>
          <span>&ldquo;We don&rsquo;t see things as they are;</span>
          <span>we see them as we are.&rdquo;</span>
          <cite>— Anaïs Nin</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="program-intro" aria-label="About the program">
        <div class="program-intro-inner">
          <p class="program-row program-row-left">Equal parts community immersion and time in the mountains. It was designed by looking at what tourists usually do in Sikkim and inviting people into life beyond the overcrowded tourist circuit.</p>
          <p class="program-row program-row-right">Ease yourself into how life is lived in Yuksom. The days are unhurried, but your hands are busy and your heart is full. Churn butter, forage in the forest, learn the dying art of bamboo weaving.</p>
          <p class="program-row program-row-center">Let your curiosities guide the way you interact with the land, the people, and yourself.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="community-quote" aria-label="Community introduction">
        <blockquote>Meet the community who fought against a commercial company from exploiting the land.</blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-section-place">
        <div class="story-media image-placeholder tall"><span>Yuksom / community photograph</span></div>
        <div class="story-copy">
          <h2>Where you’ll be</h2>
          <p>Yuksom, West Sikkim. Gateway to the Khangchendzonga mountain range. Home to a community whose values have been shaped by the mountains they call home.</p>
          <p>Learn from the farmers, craftspeople, neighbours, aunties and uncles you will meet during the journey.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-section-reflection">
        <div class="story-copy">
          <h2>Hold space for yourself</h2>
          <p>Reflection sharpens your awareness of your inner world. Don’t underestimate the power of collective energy that comes from being in a supportive environment (even just for 11 days).</p>
          <p>Allow yourself to be seen. Be heard.</p>
          <p>Reflection will be an ongoing part of your journey. There will be intentional time+space for you to notice what is happening around you and what is happening within you.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="story-media image-placeholder tall"><span>Reflection / quiet moment photograph</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="section rhythm">
        <div class="wide">
          <h2>The rhythm of The Flagship</h2>
          <div class="days-grid">
            <article><span>Day 1</span><h3>Arrival & Welcome</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 2</span><h3>Knowing our roots</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 3</span><h3>Everything is related (through the lens of Phurki)</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 4</span><h3>Soil biome is our gut biome</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 5</span><h3>Wildcard / Free & easy day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 6</span><h3>Start of trek</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 7</span><h3>Summit day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 8</span><h3>Rest day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 9</span><h3>Descend day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 10</span><h3>Nature therapy</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 11</span><h3>Not Goodbye</h3></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section editorial-grid">
        <div class="copy-block">
          <h2>Where you will stay</h2>
          <p>Ejam Residency is a three-storey home in Yuksom. The Ejam family who built it lives on the second floor.</p>
          <p>You’ll know the moment you step inside why I insist on putting Feels Like Om guests here.</p>
          <p>There are private bathrooms, hot showers, laundry (and WIFI) — but past guests speak much more fondly of conversations shared with the family in the kitchen, mornings on the lawn, afternoons in the library, and one guest’s favourite morning ritual: taking huge mouthfuls of fresh air from the balcony.</p>
          <p>You are staying in a homestay, but more importantly, you are staying in a home.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="image-stack">
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Ejam Residency photograph</span></div>
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Room / kitchen / lawn photograph</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section food-grid">
        <div class="image-stack">
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Breakfast photograph</span></div>
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Dinner / local food photograph</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <h2>What you will eat</h2>
          <p>Breakfast is a grand affair.</p>
          <p>Durga decides what you’re eating. It might be buckwheat pancakes one morning and <em>alu puri</em> the next. There is always fresh fruit, hot tea and coffee, and the spread changes daily.</p>
          <p class="pull">Dinner is omakase.</p>
          <p>The cooking here isn’t what you find in restaurants. What’s served on the dinner table depends on what’s grown and what Durga feels like cooking!</p>
          <p><em>Cowrie.</em> Hand-rolled gnochhi-like dumplings in hot, savoury soup.</p>
          <p><em>Thentuk.</em> Hand-pulled noodles in hot, savoury soup.</p>
          <p><em>Iskus ko munta.</em> The first shoots of the chayote — a plant that keeps giving.</p>
          <p><em>Pumpkin soup from the garden.</em> Durga’s secret recipe.</p>
          <p><em>Nettle soup. Fiddlehead ferns. Edible flowers.</em> Depends on seasonal availability.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mountain-chapter">
        <div class="mountain-hero image-placeholder dark-placeholder"><span>Mountain trek photograph</span></div>
        <div class="section mountain-copy">
          <div class="narrow">
            <p class="eyebrow">Into the mountains</p>
            <h2>About the trek</h2>
            <p>This is not a crowded trek, and we hope to keep it this way.</p>
            <p class="pull light-pull">You’ll see more mountains than people.</p>
            <p>The trails are well-marked and regularly maintained by villagers who use them when they bring their <em>dzos</em> into the forest to graze. <em>Dzos are hybrids of cows and yaks.</em></p>
            <p>The few days we spend in Yuksom before the trek will give your body time to gradually adjust to the altitude before we head higher.</p>
            <p>For first-timers to mountainous areas at higher altitude, people generally get breathless more quickly than usual when walking uphill or climbing stairs.</p>
            <p>A separate acclimatisation period is generally not required.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section permission">
          <div class="narrow center">
            <h2>Before we enter</h2>
            <p class="pull light-pull">Before we begin the trek, we ask the mountains for permission to enter.</p>
            <p>When we return, we thank them for safe passage.</p>
            <p>We invite you to observe respectfully. Participation is welcomed, but optional.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section trek-practical">
          <div class="wide practical-grid">
            <article>
              <h3>Your trekking team</h3>
              <p>One guide leads from the front and an assistant guide walks at the back as sweeper.</p>
              <p>Behind the scenes, a full team of guides, cooks, dzos and their handlers keeps the trek moving.</p>
              <p>You only need to carry a light daypack with your water bottle, an extra layer and whatever you need for the day.</p>
              <p>The dzos will carry the rest for us, which is also why we ask you to pack only what you genuinely need. The dzos are very strong but we also hope to keep their load as light as possible.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>How comfortable is camping?</h3>
              <p>Your well-being is very important to us.</p>
              <p>A-frame tents, four-inch mattresses, down sleeping bags, camping pillows, fleece blankets, hot water bags, a dining tent with tables and chairs, a toilet tent with a commode, and multi-course meals three times a day.</p>
              <p class="pull small-pull">Everything else, the mountains shall provide.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Do I need to be an experienced trekker?</h3>
              <p>Prior hiking experience will help, but it isn’t strictly necessary.</p>
              <p>One of our dear guests celebrated her 50th birthday with us — and this was her first ever trek.</p>
              <p>Expect steep stretches for much of the trek. We take short breaks along the way and move at a pace the group can sustain.</p>
              <p>A reasonable level of fitness and stamina is required. Technical climbing skills are not required, but good balance — especially while descending rocky terrain — is a huge bonus. We can lend you trekking poles.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section fit-section">
        <div class="narrow">
          <h2>Who this journey is for</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>You would rather stay somewhere longer than hop around places</li>
            <li>You are curious about how other people live</li>
            <li>You like the outdoors</li>
            <li>You want to experience culture as a participant rather than a spectator</li>
            <li>You are willing to let go of a little control</li>
            <li>You are willing to bring your own questions and curiosities</li>
            <li>You want space to think and reflect</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section difference">
        <div class="wide two-col">
          <div>
            <h2>How is this different?</h2>
            <p>The Flagship was not designed by looking at what tourists usually do in Sikkim and rearranging the itinerary.</p>
            <p>We keep our groups small because intimacy matters when we want to build connection and make meaning.</p>
            <p>We don’t do sightseeing because more than being a “beautiful view” and “interesting culture”, we have much to learn from the mountains and the people.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2>How do the Sikkimese people benefit?</h2>
            <p>A significant part of what you are paying for is not an “activity”.</p>
            <p>It is people’s time, energy, and wisdom. Local families, guides, cooks, craftspeople, drivers and community members. The financial exchange is but one part of the larger inter-relational exchange that takes place with your intentional presence.</p>
            <p>We work with people who live in and around the places we visit so that the economic benefit remains within the community.</p>
            <p>We also care about how people are represented, who controls the narrative, and whether your presence protects their way of life instead of eroding it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section glance">
        <div class="wide">
          <h2>At a glance</h2>
          <div class="glance-grid">
            <div><span>Journey</span><strong>11 days / 10 nights</strong></div>
            <div><span>Trek</span><strong>4 days / 3 nights</strong></div>
            <div><span>Highest point</span><strong>3,340m</strong></div>
            <div><span>Group</span><strong>2–6</strong></div>
            <div><span>Stay</span><strong>7 nights at Ejam Residency</strong></div>
            <div><span>Preparation</span><strong>Pre-trip + pre-trek briefing</strong></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section reflections">
        <div class="wide">
          <h2>Reflections from past participants</h2>
          <div class="reflection-grid">
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
          </div>
          <a class="text-link" href="/reflections">Read more reflections →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="enquire" class="section enquire">
        <div class="narrow center">
          <h2>Curious about The Flagship?</h2>
          <p>If you want to understand the journey in more detail, tell us you’re interested.</p>
          <p>We’ll send you the full Flagship brochure with the detailed itinerary, trek information, inclusions, preparation notes and everything else you need to decide whether this journey is for you.</p>
          <a class="button" href="/contact">Enquire about The Flagship →</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
